entry.summary:
Stop Using the Singleton Pattern
The Singleton pattern looks simple and useful. It is also one of the most common causes of hidden design problems in software systems. Singleton ensures that a class has only one instance and that this instance can be accessed globally. Logger.getInstance().log("Hello");
One instance. Global access. Singleton is just global state. Any part of the code can access it, change it, and depend on it without being explicit. This makes systems harder to understand, test, and change. Singleton often causes: Hidden dependencies Difficult unit testing State leaking across requests Tight coupling between classes What looks convenient early becomes technical debt later. Instead of accessing global objects, pass dependencies explicitly. This makes dependencies visible, code easier to test, and systems more flexible. If passing a dependency feels annoying, the solution is better design — not a Singleton.
